# Design: dstl sparse alltoall

Sparse personalized all-to-all: every rank sends messages to a (small, dynamic) set of
destinations, and no rank knows in advance from whom it will receive. Implements the four
protocols of Hoefler, Siebert, Lumsdaine, *Scalable Communication Protocols for Dynamic Sparse
Data Exchange* (PPoPP 2010), selected by a tag. Unlike `MPI_Alltoallv`, NBX has communication
complexity linear in the number of messages actually sent, not in the communicator size.

---

## Primitives

| Primitive | Status |
|---|---|
| `probe_result`, `v2::mprobe`, `v2::improbe` | done (#83) |
| `v2::mrecv`, `v2::imrecv` (take `probe_result` by value, size deferred buffers via `infer(comm_op::mrecv, ...)`) | done (#85) |
| `ibarrier` (v2 returns a `request`) | done (#84) |
| `request_vector`, `testall` | done (#82) |
| `reduce_scatter_block` | missing — needed for PCX |
| one-sided windows (`MPI_Win`, `MPI_Accumulate`) | missing — needed for RSX |

---

## Algorithm selection

Tags in the style of the existing `execution_policy::*` / `layout::*` tags (`tags.hpp`):

```cpp
namespace dstl::sparse_alltoall_algorithm {
struct nbx {}; // nonblocking consensus: issend + improbe + ibarrier
struct pex {}; // personalized exchange: alltoall of counts
struct pcx {}; // personalized census: reduce_scatter of message flags
struct rsx {}; // remote summing: MPI_Accumulate into a counter window
}
template <typename T>
concept is_sparse_alltoall_algorithm = /* one of the tags above */;
```

The tag is the first argument (like `std::execution` policies). Every entry point has an
overload without it that defaults to `nbx`.

---

## Layer 1 — callback primitive

```cpp
template <
    is_sparse_alltoall_algorithm                            Algorithm,
    sparse_send_buffer                                      Sends,
    std::invocable<incoming_message>p                        OnMessage,
    mpi::experimental::convertible_to_mpi_handle<MPI_Comm>  Comm = MPI_Comm>
void dstl::sparse_alltoall(Algorithm, Sends&& sends, OnMessage&& on_message, Comm const& comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD);
```

### Input: `sparse_send_buffer`

A range of `(dest, inner_range)` pairs — the existing `sparse_nested_send_buffer` (`payload.hpp`),
plus the requirement that each inner range is `contiguous_range && sized_range`, since inner
ranges are sent directly without copying. Inner ranges are plain ranges (`std::vector<T>`,
`std::span<T>`, …); they need not be `send_buffer`s, so payload types without a builtin MPI type
work (their type comes from the value-type channel below).

### Datatype: the value-type channel

The payload datatype is attached once to the whole structure, not per message:

```cpp
dstl::sparse_alltoall(sends | kamping::v2::views::with_value_pool(p), on_message, comm);
dstl::sparse_alltoall(sends | kamping::v2::views::with_value_type(dt), on_message, comm);
dstl::sparse_alltoall(sends, on_message, comm); // builtin payload: value_type() fallback
```

The implementation reads `kamping::v2::value_type(sends)` once (as `request_reply` does) and uses it
for every send. Consequences:

- One datatype per call, by construction.
- The datatype must outlive the call; with `with_value_pool` the `type_pool` guarantees this.
- No per-message views are created just to carry a type.

### Callback: `incoming_message`

The protocols know different things about an incoming message: PEX knows source and size up
front, NBX/PCX only learn about a message by probing it. If the callback always received a
`probe_result`, PEX would have to probe every message anyway and lose part of its advantage. So
the callback receives an algorithm-independent handle (name tentative):

```cpp
class incoming_message {
public:
    int source() const;
    template <typename T> std::optional<int> count() const;  // elements of T, nullopt for MPI_UNDEFINED
    auto recv(RBuf&& rbuf) &&;                                // blocking; goes through infer()
    auto irecv(RBuf&& rbuf) &&;                               // -> iresult<RBuf>
    auto irecv(Request&& request, RBuf&& rbuf) &&;            // caller-supplied request
};
```

- NBX / PCX: wraps a `probe_result`; `recv` / `irecv` are `v2::mrecv` / `v2::imrecv`.
- PEX: holds `(source, count)`; `recv` / `irecv` are a targeted `recv` / `irecv`, sizing deferred
  buffers from the known count.

`recv` / `irecv` size deferred buffers (`views::resize`, …) from the count, computed with the
*receive* buffer's datatype — so only the sizes of sent and received elements have to match, not
the declared types.

Contract: move-only, received exactly once; dropping an unreceived message terminates, like
`mpi::experimental::message` (for NBX / PCX this falls out of `probe_result`; the PEX handle needs
its own flag). The callback may move the handle out and receive it later, as long as that happens
before `sparse_alltoall` returns.

**Open:** PEX exchanges counts in units of the send datatype. Either convert to the receive
buffer's datatype via the type sizes, or require the sizes to match.

---

## Shared send phase

All four protocols start by sending every `(dest, inner)` pair. This lives in one place,
parameterized by the send mode:

- NBX uses **`issend`** (see below); PEX, PCX and RSX use `isend`.
- Requests go into a `request_vector`; inner ranges are sent as `(data, size, value_type)`.
- Owning per-message views (e.g. `views::serialize` applied via `std::views::transform`) would be
  destroyed while their send is in flight. For non-`borrowed_range` inner types, the send phase
  moves each one into an internal vector that lives until all sends complete; for borrowed inner
  types this is compiled away. Not implemented yet — until then, materialize first:

  ```cpp
  auto staged = sends
      | std::views::transform([](auto& msg) { auto& [dst, data] = msg; return std::pair{dst, data | kamping::views::serialize}; })
      | std::ranges::to<std::vector>();
  dstl::sparse_alltoall(staged, callback, comm);
  ```

---

## Protocols

### NBX

```
1. issend every message (synchronous mode!), collecting requests
2. loop:
       if (auto probe = improbe(MPI_ANY_SOURCE, tag, comm))
           on_message(incoming_message{std::move(*probe)})
       if (!barrier_started && testall(send_requests))
           barrier = ibarrier(comm); barrier_started = true
       if (barrier_started && barrier.test()) break
```

The sends **must be synchronous** (`issend`): termination relies on a completed send meaning the
receiver has matched the message, which is what allows a rank to enter the `ibarrier` once all its
sends are complete. With `isend`, a small message can complete locally before it is matched, and
the protocol can terminate with messages still in flight.

### PEX

`alltoall` of per-destination counts (O(p) per rank), then post the receives for every non-zero
count and send. Sizes are known up front, so no probing; delivery order is predictable.

### PCX

`reduce_scatter_block` of a 0/1 "I send to you" vector yields the number of incoming messages;
then `mprobe(MPI_ANY_SOURCE, tag)` that many times. Needs a `reduce_scatter_block` wrapper
(small, follows the existing collectives pattern).

### RSX

Each sender `MPI_Accumulate`s +1 into a counter on each destination within a fence epoch, yielding
the number of incoming messages; then probe as in PCX. Needs one-sided windows, which mpi-core does
not have yet — either a minimal window wrapper or raw MPI inside dstl.

---

## Message isolation across calls

All protocols send on a reserved tag. After its barrier completes, a fast rank can start the next
`sparse_alltoall` and send while a slower rank is still probing in the previous one and may match
the new message. **Open:** pick one mechanism for all protocols:

- a final `barrier()` (as in the original sketch) — simple, costs a collective per call;
- a duplicated communicator per call (or cached per communicator);
- tags alternating between consecutive calls.

---

## Layer 2 — flat buffer convenience

Built on Layer 1. Takes a variadic send buffer (flat data + per-rank counts + displacements) and
only sends to ranks with non-zero counts. Its input is already flat, so plain `with_type` applies.

```cpp
template <
    is_sparse_alltoall_algorithm                            Algorithm,
    mpi::experimental::data_buffer_v                        SBuf,
    mpi::experimental::recv_buffer                          RBuf,
    is_output_layout                                        Layout = layout::unordered,
    mpi::experimental::convertible_to_mpi_handle<MPI_Comm>  Comm   = MPI_Comm>
void dstl::sparse_alltoall(Algorithm, SBuf&& sbuf, RBuf&& rbuf, Layout = {}, Comm const& comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD);
```

NBX / PCX / RSX deliver in arrival order. The output order reuses the existing layout tags:
`layout::unordered` (default) appends in arrival order; `layout::ordered_by_source` yields the
`MPI_Alltoallv` layout (grouped by increasing source rank).

---

## Higher-level convenience variants (built on Layer 1)

```cpp
// Collect all received messages as (source, data) pairs
template <typename T, ...>
auto sparse_alltoall(Sends&&, Comm const&) -> std::vector<std::pair<int, std::vector<T>>>;

// Flat: all elements concatenated + per-element source annotation
template <typename T, ...>
auto sparse_alltoall(Sends&&, Comm const&) -> std::pair<std::vector<T>, std::vector<int>>;
```

Both are thin wrappers over the callback primitive, and accept the algorithm tag like it.

---

## Dropped: `transform_messages`

The original design had `sends | dstl::views::transform_messages(adaptor)` to apply a view to every
message, mainly to attach a datatype per message (`with_auto_pool(p)`, `with_type(dt)`). The
value-type channel (`with_value_type` / `with_value_pool`, see [`value_type.md`](value_type.md))
replaces that with a single whole-buffer annotation. Remaining per-message transformations that
change the data (e.g. `views::serialize`) are written with `std::views::transform` on the pairs —
`transform_messages` was only shorthand for it. The owning-view lifetime issue is independent of
the spelling and handled in the shared send phase.

---

## Implementation order

1. Shared send phase + `incoming_message` + NBX (all primitives available).
2. PEX (needs only `alltoall`).
3. PCX, after a `reduce_scatter_block` wrapper.
4. RSX, after a minimal window wrapper (or raw MPI inside dstl).
5. Layer 2 and the convenience variants.
